        heading: "Pier Foundations, Grouting, and Water Control",
        body: "Drilled shafts are the typical karst foundation. The engineer sizes the shaft for the structural loads, specifies a rock socket length into verified competent rock, and requires probe drilling below each shaft tip, usually one to two diameters deep, to confirm no void hides beneath. Shaft inspection is rigorous: the engineer or inspector verifies rock bearing visually or with test methods, checks socket cleanliness, and confirms concrete placement, because a shaft tipped on a thin rock shelf over a void is a collapse waiting for a load. Where rock is too deep or too variable, the design may switch to grouted ground improvement plus a mat, but only with verification.\n\nGrouting treats the voids the investigation found. Compaction grouting densifies loose soil zones, while permeation grouting fills open rock voids with cementitious grout; the engineer designs the grout mix, injection pressures, and verification program, including post-grout borings or geophysics proving the voids are filled. Permanent water control is designed in parallel: roofs and paving drain to lined channels or pipes that carry water off the karst area, detention is lined or relocated, and utility trenches get clay dams so they do not become underground streams feeding the voids. The drawings show these as civil and structural details, because karst performance is a site system, not a single foundation element.",
