    title: "How Are Lake-Effect Snow Loads Calculated for Cleveland Roofs?",
    description: "Cleveland roof snow loads start from ASCE 7 ground snow values, adjusted upward for Lake Erie lake-effect snow, then shaped by slope, drifting, and unbalanced loading — engineered so the structure handles the worst drifted case.",
    h1: "How Are Lake-Effect Snow Loads Calculated for Cleveland Roofs?",
    answer: "Direct answer: lake-effect snow loads for Cleveland roofs are calculated by starting with the ASCE 7 ground snow load for the site, applying the code's lake-effect increase for locations near the Great Lakes, and then converting that ground load into balanced, unbalanced, and drifted roof loads using factors for roof slope, exposure, thermal condition, and importance. The structural engineer checks drifting at parapets, roof steps, and rooftop units — where wind piles snow far deeper than the uniform depth — and designs the roof structure for the worst-case combination. The calculations are part of the structural permit set reviewed by the City of Cleveland's building department.\n\nLake-effect snow is what separates Cleveland from inland Ohio. Cold air sweeping across the relatively warm waters of Lake Erie picks up moisture and dumps it as intense, localized snow bands on the downwind shore — Cleveland's east side and the Snowbelt communities can receive dramatically more snow than areas just a few miles inland. ASCE 7 recognizes this with a ground snow load increase for sites near the Great Lakes, and the engineer applies it before any roof conversion factors. The result is a design ground snow load meaningfully higher than what the same building would require fifty miles south.\n\nConverting ground snow to roof load is where the engineering detail lives. The engineer applies the exposure factor for the site's terrain, the thermal factor for heated versus unheated spaces below the roof, and the importance factor for the building's occupancy — a hospital or school carries a higher factor than a storage building. Sloped roofs shed some load through the slope factor, but the engineer must also check the unbalanced case: wind scouring snow off the windward slope and piling it on the leeward side, which can more than double the load on one side of a gable or arched roof. These are not refinements — unbalanced and drift loads routinely control the design of individual members.\n\nDrifting deserves its own attention because it creates the deepest, most concentrated loads. At parapets, penthouses, roof steps where a high roof meets a low roof, and around rooftop equipment, wind-deposited drifts form triangular surcharges the engineer calculates from the upwind fetch distance and the ground snow load. The engineer checks every drift location on the roof plan, designs the affected joists, beams, and columns for the surcharge, and details the roof drainage so meltwater does not pond behind drifted snow. In Cleveland's building department review, snow load calculations with drift diagrams are a standard plan-review checkpoint, and the authority having jurisdiction decides approvals — the engineer's job is a complete, traceable load path from snowflake to foundation.",
    directAnswer: "Cleveland roof snow loads are calculated from ASCE 7 ground snow loads, increased by a lake-effect factor that accounts for the heavy, frequent snow off Lake Erie, then adjusted for roof slope, exposure, drifting at parapets and roof steps, and unbalanced loading, with the structure designed for the worst-case drifted condition.",
