    answer: "Direct answer: a parking garage in Indianapolis is structurally designed as a cast-in-place or precast concrete frame with floor slabs — often post-tensioned — engineered for vehicle live loads, lateral wind and seismic forces, and long-term durability against deicing salts and freeze-thaw cycles. The engineer lays out the column grid around parking efficiency and vehicle turning paths, designs the slabs for gravity and the lateral system for stability, details drainage so salt-laden water never ponds on the structure, and specifies corrosion protection for every embedded element. The structural set goes through the City of Indianapolis Department of Business and Neighborhood Services for sites inside the consolidated city, or the relevant county or town for sites outside it.\n\nThe structural system starts with the parking layout, because structure and parking efficiency are inseparable. Bay sizes are set by the parking module — typically spanning two rows of stalls plus the drive aisle — and the engineer selects the floor system that spans those bays economically: post-tensioned cast-in-place slabs, conventionally reinforced slabs, or precast double tees. Post-tensioning is common in Indianapolis garages because it produces thin, crack-controlled slabs with long spans and fewer columns, which means more parking spaces and better sightlines. The engineer designs for the vehicle live loads the code prescribes, including the concentrated wheel loads that control punching shear at columns, and checks vibration so the floors do not feel lively under traffic.\n\nDurability design is where Indianapolis winters enter the calculations. Every winter, vehicles track deicing salts onto the decks, and chloride-laden water is the primary enemy of a parking structure's lifespan. The engineer specifies low-permeability concrete, adequate cover, corrosion-resistant reinforcement or post-tensioning tendon protection, and penetrating sealers or traffic-bearing membranes on the top deck. Drainage is engineered, not assumed: slabs slope to drains, drains are placed to eliminate ponding, and the engineer details drip edges and joint seals so water does not migrate through the structure. Expansion joints accommodate thermal movement across the large, exposed floor plates.\n\nThe lateral system must handle an open, exposed frame. Parking garages have few interior walls to hide shear walls in, so the engineer typically designs the frame itself — moment frames, shear walls at stairs and elevator cores, or a combination — for wind and seismic loads per ASCE 7. Foundation design follows the geotechnical report for the site's soils. Permitting runs through the city's Department of Business and Neighborhood Services for locations within the consolidated city-county, while sites in the surrounding donut counties go through those counties' building departments — same structural engineering, different submittal portals, review timelines, and inspection scheduling, which the engineer confirms before the permit set is sealed.",
