        heading: "Elevation and Flood-Resistant Construction",
        body: "For new riverfront construction, elevation is the most robust strategy. The engineer sets the lowest floor above the design flood elevation with the freeboard the ordinance requires, and designs the supporting structure — whether fill, stem walls, or piers — for the flood forces at the site. Enclosed areas below the elevated floor, such as parking or storage, are built with flood openings sized to the enclosed area so water flows in and out automatically; the engineer calculates the required net open area and details the openings so they cannot be blocked by finishes or stored materials.\n\nEverything below the flood elevation is built to get wet and survive. The engineer specifies flood-resistant materials — concrete, masonry, metal, pressure-treated lumber, closed-cell insulation — and prohibits gypsum board, fiberglass batts, and other materials that floodwater destroys. Electrical panels, transformers, boilers, and elevator equipment go above the flood level; where that is impossible, they go in floodproof enclosures designed for submersion. The site grading directs water away from the building where possible, and the engineer coordinates the floodproofing with the architect so elevated entries, ramps, and flood openings read as intentional design rather than afterthoughts.",
