        heading: "Chicago's Review and Overlapping Authorities",
        body: "Building permits for riverfront floodproofing go through the Chicago Department of Buildings, with structural drawings, flood elevation documentation, and — for dry-floodproofing — the professional certification that the design resists the flood forces. Where the site lies in the mapped floodplain, the floodplain requirements add elevation certificates, floodproofing certificates, and compliance with the city's floodplain ordinance to the submittal. The authority having jurisdiction decides approvals, and reviewers check the flood load path with the same seriousness as the gravity and wind systems.\n\nRiverfront parcels can trigger overlapping reviews. Work near the waterway may involve the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers for in-water or shoreline work, and sewer separation or discharge questions can involve the Metropolitan Water Reclamation District. The engineer identifies every applicable authority at project start, because a floodproofing design approved by the Department of Buildings still needs its shoreline or sewer components cleared by the other agencies. Submittal portals, review timelines, and inspection requirements differ across these authorities, so the permitting strategy sequences them to avoid a late objection from an agency that was not consulted early.",
